/// Whether a kept state still restores for a prompt of `len` tokens: live, not stale, a chunk end before the
/// prompt's replay (the rows before it ran the encoder layers alone, as a fresh pass of this prompt runs them).
fn usable(self: *const Lanes, sn: *const Snap, len: usize) bool {
    if (std.mem.indexOfScalar(*Snap, self.snaps.items, @constCast(sn)) == null) return false;
    if (self.kv) |k| if (k.planner.keptIndex(sn.id) == null) return false; // (TF_DS_KV_SHARD: its rows the planner holds)
    if (sn.stale or sn.loading or sn.at == 0) return false;
    if (sn.at == len) return sn.is_end; // (0178: an identical resent prompt's full end state: zero rows)
    if (sn.at > len) return false;
    // 0180 (TF_DS_TAIL_BATCH): an end state continues a longer prompt too - its rings hold the window, so the
    // pass computes the new rows only (today's states keep no complete rings and every warm pass replays the
    // window). Sequential vs a cold pass: the rows' chunk shapes differ (W8's finding; 0121's class).
    // A resumed prompt chunk starts with a complete positional group in ratio-2 KV layers.
    // Identical odd ends return above without running a chunk; appended turns use an earlier hit.
    if (sn.is_end) return self.m.tail_batch != null and sn.at % 2 == 0;
    // TF_DS_EXACT_RESUME: an off-grid state nearer the end than the window resumes on its tail's rows
    if (sn.tail != null) return tailPre(sn, len, self.m.cfg.window) != null;
    return sn.at % model.chunk_rows == 0 and sn.at <= len -| self.m.cfg.window;
}

/// TF_DS_EXACT_RESUME: the rows a pass resuming from a tail-carrying state at a prompt of `len` tokens takes from
/// that tail before the state's position (null: the tail does not hold the replay's rows, so it runs from 0).
fn tailPre(sn: *const Snap, len: usize, window: usize) ?usize {
    if (sn.at == 0 or sn.at >= len) return null;
    const replay = len -| window;
    if (sn.tail_start > replay or sn.tail_end < sn.at) return null;
    return sn.at -| replay;
}

/// The first extent that fits around the live streams and the kept states. Kept states give their room oldest
/// first and `source` (the state this prompt resumes from, its rows copied here) last, as the Python lane's _place
/// takes the oldest other kept prompt before the source; a state whose rows lie in a live stream's extent gives
/// none (that stream holds the room) and stays. When a live stream holds `source`, the request first waits up to
/// held_wait_ns for that stream to end (error.ContextFull: the host tries it again; it then resumes in place) if
/// the copy needs another state's room or would leave none for another request of its size: a copy that fills the
/// pool makes the next prompt evict a kept state (the 5 x 500K check with the duplicate admitted fourth). Kept
/// states hold their stream's whole extent back while a placement still fits so (placeSoft): its tail stays free
/// for the resume in place, which then stales nothing. With TF_DS_KEEP_PROTECT=1 the room comes first from a snap
/// keptOver counts as superseded (any overlapping snap on a different base, or a longer one on its own base), then
/// the oldest non-shared snap, then the oldest shared one.
fn placeKept(self: *Lanes, live: []const [2]usize, size: usize, source: ?*Snap) !usize {

    for (self.snaps.items) |sn| if (sn != except and sn.base < hi and lo < sn.base + sn.at) {
        if (!sn.stale) {
            noteStale(sn, "a stream writes over its rows");
            if (self.spill) |t| t.guardSnap(sn.id, @intCast(@max(lo, sn.base) - sn.base));
        }
        sn.stale = true;
    };
}

