Zig 0.17's Type.Struct uses `field_names` / `field_types` arrays. So iterate:

```zig
const T = @typeInfo(model.Snapshot).@"struct";
inline for (T.field_names, T.field_types, 0..) |name, FieldType, idx| {
    _ = idx;
    try expectEqual(c.cfg.name, step, name, @field(want, name), @field(got, name));
}
```
Hmm — `@field(want, name)` with comptime name ✓. Field name type is `[:0]const u8` ✓ works as []const u8 for print.

Simpler alternative: skip reflection entirely — compare a tuple or just list the fields explicitly (6 fields). Explicit is clearer and avoids reflection API churn:

```zig
fn checkState(c: *Ctx, step: usize, find_on: []const u32) !void {
    const want = c.model.snapshot(find_on);
    const got = c.kernelSnapshot(find_on);
    try expectEqual(c.cfg.name, step, "entries", want.entries, got.entries);
    try expectEqual(c.cfg.name, step, "kept", want.kept, got.kept);
    try expectEqual(c.cfg.name, step, "saves", want.saves, got.saves);
    try expectEqual(c.cfg.name, step, "find_at", want.find_at, got.find_at);
    try expectEqual(c.cfg.name, step, "full_end", want.full_end, got.full_end);
    try expectEqual(c.cfg.name, step, "has_point", want.has_point, got.has_point);
}
```
Explicit — ponytail (and robust). Do that.

Also expectEqual's print `model={} store={}` with ?u32 — check std.fmt optional handling... `{}` on ?u32: In Zig 0.17, formatAny — optional with `{}`... I believe it errors at compile time ("cannot format optional without a specifier")? Hmm — actually I recall that `{}` on optionals works in newer Zig (formats null as "null"). Let me not gamble: use `{?}`... but then non-optional fields with `{?}` — `{?}` on non-optional? Also questionable. Hmm.

Actually std.fmt's formatType: for optional types with `{}`... let me grep the fmt code quickly. Or avoid the issue: expectEqual compares and prints with `{any}`? `{any}` handles everything (optionals print as optional.?). `{any}` on ?u32 prints `optional(5)` or `null` — fine for a diagnostic line. Use `{any}` in expectEqual's print. And in the mark step print `m.f` with `{}` — a struct... `{}` on a struct works if it has no custom formatter — std.fmt auto-formats structs with `{}`? Hmm — actually for plain structs `{}` works (default format prints fields) — I think yes in 0.17 (`{}` falls back to `{any}`-ish for structs... ). To be safe use `{any}` there too.

